/-!
# Exact source comparison integration

The direct and prescribed-smoothing Weil comparisons retain the independently
defined arithmetic form, the strict cutoff, every analytic-multiplicity slot,
and ordinary complex squares. Both proof candidates belong to the checked
import graph; successful pinned verification and review remain acceptance gates.
The exponential adapters keep the exact comparison and the newly named estimate
explicit. The signed lower estimate is separate. No unconditional RH theorem is asserted.

The signed-estimate additions bound the analytic remainder, the proper-prime-power
sector, and an exponential envelope, preserving the actual correlation and cutoff.
The remaining polynomial cancellation estimate is not inferred from these bounds.

The truncated Möbius identity is specialized to every original source index.
The weighted negative-part integral is proved at rates greater than one;
integrability at every positive rate remains an explicitly named open target.

The two actual exponential moments and derivative energy give a signed sampling
bound and a polynomial Möbius small-divisor budget. The full complementary sum
is retained exactly; its required global cancellation is not inferred.

The source derivative and full closed-right Mertens formula preserve the lower
endpoint atom. Its polynomial budget isolates the entire signed integral.
The actual coprime-ratio identity preserves Möbius zeros and signs, without
providing the missing estimate for the growing complementary ratio classes.

The full finite quadratic transport retains both ordered Möbius coefficients.
The actual second exponential moment and a compact causal primitive supply
analytic sampling inputs. They do not assert positivity of the complete
arithmetic form or cancellation in its full off-diagonal and linear combination.

The complete prime-supported gcd blocks have a finite-product bound. Primes
through (log A)^2 give a polynomial bound and genuine all-positive-rate
integrability of that extracted block, with the original normalization. The
complementary rough ratios and full linear term remain in an exact identity;
their required combined negative-part estimate remains open.

Finite squarefree counting and exact Abel summation separate every actual gcd
class into its finite density mean and weighted variation error. These are
assembled over the entire rough complement in the original Q identity. The
mean and variation sums remain explicit; the all-rate bound for their combined
signed arithmetic is not assumed or supplied by the counting theorem.

The exact historical WeilSource modules are reused under the current pins and
audited separately. The actual normalized test's weak second derivative has
finite energy, giving the continuous third correlation derivative and its
inverse-cubic bound. The sampling kernel differentiates twice across its moving
strict cutoff. Its signed derivative kernel retains the negative second-moment
coefficient; these analytic inputs do not control the entire arithmetic sum.

The actual real-kernel remainder has an inverse-three-halves derivative bound.
It gives a uniform bound for each complete finite class variation, including
its endpoint and empty-class case, with no derivative estimate premise. Exact
one-prime insertion retains both rectangular cutoffs and every Mobius sign in
the original supported block. Cancellation across the full outer means and
errors together with the original linear term remains a separate open task.
Prime exhaustion identifies the complete supported block with the full
arithmetic remainder. A separate generic counterexample prevents inferring
prime-filter contraction from decay exponents alone; it is not a replacement
for, or counterexample to, the actual source kernel.
Prime two retains an explicit odd-scale rectangle boundary and the complete
even-scale term. The original linear sum has its own odd upper band; its
transport into Q preserves every rough mean and signed remainder. The proved
sampling difference bound still has inverse-square-root decay and supplies no
all-positive-rate estimate for the complete expression.

An actual-cutoff coefficient guard rejects coefficientwise positivity of the
truncated Dirichlet square and retains its exact cancellation with the linear
coefficient. A weighted L1 premise for the original negative part gives genuine
complex Laplace integrability and holomorphy to its right. This analytic
transport does not prove that arithmetic premise at any improved decay rate.
The actual finite squarefree density has signed two-insertion cancellation,
including the reciprocal-square tail and both square-root cutoffs. The full
kernel-weighted mean still needs a separate transport and estimate. An exact
joint parity identity retains the original linear band and both quadratic
boundary sectors without asserting positivity of that joint expression.

The actual homogeneity defect has an inverse-radius coefficient, yielding a
logarithmic scale comparison by genuine derivative integration. Its transport
into the three actual finite-density class means preserves both inserted
cutoffs, two scale-difference sums, and two strict-lower boundary tails. The
closed per-class error estimate applies at common-scale cutoff at least eight;
the growing smaller-cutoff region remains. These comparisons do not establish
signed cancellation across all means, class-counting errors, and the original
linear term, or improve the complete negative-part integrability rate.

Actual finite Mobius inversion cancels the complete upper linear band against
the upper quadratic rows. The unchanged source is the retained smaller core
minus two original upper channels. An exact product-fiber Abel transport gives
the same boundary as a genuinely integrable signed cumulative pairing, with
both endpoint atoms proved zero. Its source cumulative coefficient takes both
signs; neither its positivity nor a weighted boundary sign is inferred.
The actual upper observation is a finite source-minus-shifted-core difference.
Both channel reflection integrals are bounded by its genuine local square
integral on [0, log 2]. The remaining joint core and boundary estimate is open;
the core keeps its original scale, and the local square is not already bounded
at all positive Laplace rates.

The actual test and its derivative have proved endpoint signs for A at least
three. The genuine convolution transports these signs to the sampling
derivative on the upper product range. An exact closed-cutoff hyperbola split,
with both derivative pairings genuinely integrable, retains a favorable
square and yields a lower comparison with the entire signed triangular sum.
The core and triangle still require a joint estimate. Strict source endpoint
positivity also gives an exact two-coordinate negative direction of the upper
product kernel at A = log 32. It rejects positivity on all coordinates without
asserting a sign for the fixed full Mobius quadratic value.
Finite prime-two removal collapses a complete signed odd-column row to a
finite dyadic source sum. The clipped upper row keeps its lower-column
complete-kernel term and omitted sampling tail. Those mixed rows are not
identified with the smaller core or substituted for an unweighted hyperbola
row without a further genuine integral transport.

The pinned quantitative prime number theorem now supplies an independent
arithmetic saving for the unchanged signed pairing. Its exact source adapter,
genuine split integrals and the proved analytic correction bound establish
weighted negative-part integrability at every real rate at least one,
including the boundary rate one. Every rate strictly between zero and one
still needs a new arithmetic estimate. This is not a terminal RH theorem.

The actual prime-square sector now has an exact signed Abel formula and an
unconditional inverse-square error bound about its normalized linear term
and genuine theta constant. Every logarithmic theta-error moment, tail moment,
finite threshold integral, strict endpoint transport and correlation mass is
proved for the original source. The first-prime and higher-power sectors
remain in Q; this sector asymptotic gives no complete-source rate below one.

All higher prime powers now have a genuinely convergent arithmetic constant,
a one-sided upper bound and a uniform inverse-square error. Their exact strict
source split combines with the square sector into the full proper-power
asymptotic. The proper-power error is genuinely Laplace integrable at every
positive rate, using actual scale measurability. This is an error bound, not
the requested integrability of the complete source negative part.
The exact first-prime Abel formula leaves a bounded decaying main and an
exponentially weighted signed remainder. A pointwise source comparison keeps
that centered arithmetic remainder explicit. Its negative part at rates
below one still requires an independent arithmetic estimate; a renamed
remainder assumption or its finite polynomial moments does not provide it.

The separate fixed-filter route now admits the original positive-integer
kernel in L1 and L2 through an exact signed sampling cancellation and a proved
exponential tail. Genuine norm-integral summability gives its Laplace identity
in Re z greater than one half. The repaired zeta expression keeps its nonzero
pole value and all zero occurrences. The actual half-line reference space,
exponential inner products and scalar atom-inclusive resolvent limit are
checked. Subsequent modules construct the actual TT* operator, continue its
Laplace transform through the positive half-plane with its nonzero removed
pole value, and prove the original zeros give adjoint-kernel witnesses with
strict directional resolvent floors. Joint time-cutoff and regularization
errors are controlled on the same Hilbert space.

The full controller energy now has an explicit attained minimum equal to the
actual directional resolvent mass. Genuine double-integrability transports
positive exponential controllers to the full signed source Gram formula.
A controller whose own Laplace evaluation vanishes at one half has residual
squared norm at least four ninths; this rejects that cancellation order only.
These transports do not identify the actual spectral measure, eliminate its
zero atom, or provide an independent vanishing upper estimate. Finite corrected Möbius numerators now have exact entire quotients and
local cancellation against the actual source filter. Genuine smoothed Perron
inversion and reciprocal-series interchange identify their logarithmic
normalization, including the zero endpoint and local zeta residue. These
identities do not prove an asymptotic normalization or a full residual bound.
The independent zero-energy estimate and unchanged RH remain open.
-/
